00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.890
<v NARRATOR>From Spark and Anvil and DialogueQuest — this is Prop, in chapter 1 of the DialogueQuest cast.

2
00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:14.861
<v NARRATOR>This drama uses an AI-generated voice. Voiced via Gemini 2.5.

3
00:00:14.861 --> 00:00:27.072
<v NARRATOR>Prop is a tidy red squirrel, and his paws are never, ever still — he turns a small smooth acorn over and over as he talks.

4
00:00:27.072 --> 00:00:43.283
<v NARRATOR>Above him floats a speech bubble, but between his words little picture-boxes pop up too. Each shows a tiny action: turning the acorn, brushing his tail, glancing at the door.

5
00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:57.174
<v PROP>Of course my talking has pictures in between. Nobody just *talks.* People do little things while they talk. My name is Prop. I keep the action beats — the small stuff a character does between the words.

6
00:00:57.174 --> 00:01:04.465
<v NARRATOR>He said a line, and a box showed him not looking up from the acorn at all.

7
00:01:04.465 --> 00:01:21.436
<v PROP>I said it's a nice day. But the action-box showed I wouldn't look up. So you can tell — I didn't really mean it. The little action told you more than the words did.

8
00:01:21.436 --> 00:01:32.007
<v NARRATOR>Then his paws sped up — turning, tapping, fidgeting — and his lines came quick and clipped, and the whole talk felt nervous.

9
00:01:32.007 --> 00:01:53.858
<v PROP>Now watch. Still paws, slow talk. The beats set the speed. Busy paws, busy talk. The action is the drumbeat under the words.

10
00:01:53.858 --> 00:02:05.389
<v NARRATOR>Patter felt the click — his young writers' characters were floating heads, all lines and no bodies, nothing happening.

11
00:02:05.389 --> 00:02:15.760
<v NARRATOR>In the workshop, Patter wrote a line on the board: *"I'm not upset," she said, and set her cup down very, very slowly.*

12
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:28.091
<v PROP>What does the slow cup tell you? She *was* upset. An action beat can agree with the words — or argue with them. When it argues, the reader feels the truth underneath.

13
00:02:28.091 --> 00:02:32.302
<v NARRATOR>A young writer asked how many beats to use.

14
00:02:32.302 --> 00:02:47.433
<v PROP>Not too many. A beat here and there, at the moments that matter. Save your beats for the lines where a small action says what the words can't.

15
00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:55.924
<v NARRATOR>Afterward, Patter asked, gently, why the acorn — why he always had it.

16
00:02:55.924 --> 00:03:07.535
<v PROP>When I was small, talking was hard. The words would get stuck. So I'd hold something, and turn it, and somehow the words came easier.

17
00:03:07.535 --> 00:03:20.466
<v PROP>The acorn isn't really about the acorn. It's the thing my hands do so my heart can keep talking.

18
00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:34.597
<v NARRATOR>And Patter understood then how much of what we feel lives not in our words at all, but in the quiet, busy, honest things our hands do while we try to say them.

19
